    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class (`AcceptanceBase` is the abstract base). The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the GOLD membership tier rule following the project's existing OfficeFloor function-injection conventions and the established `namesakeCount` pattern.\n\n**Change:** `membershipTier` is now `GOLD` when the owner's household (owners sharing the same `householdId`) has 3+ members after this create; otherwise the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules apply.\n\n**How each piece fits its responsibility:**\n\n1. **`Owner.getMembershipTier()`** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 the tier is still derived purely from stored state. Added a GOLD check on a new persisted `householdSize` field, evaluated before the existing SILVER/BRONZE logic. The count is a creation-time snapshot, matching how `namesakeCount` already works.\n\n2. **`CountHouseholdMembers`** (new step) \u2014 a small single-purpose function mirroring `CountNamesakes`: it records the household size (existing members sharing the assigned `householdId`, plus this new owner; 1 for a non-shared owner). It runs *after* `AssignHousehold` (which sets/back-fills the shared id) and before save, so the count is accurate. I kept this out of `AssignHousehold` to avoid turning that class into a catch-all, and it reuses the existing `householdId`/`findByLastName` machinery rather than duplicating household-matching logic.\n\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `countHouseholdMembers` into the pipeline between `assignHousehold` and `applyRegistrationDate`, under the same `transaction` governance as its neighbors.\n\n4. **Schemas** \u2014 added `household_size` to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) to match the new `@Column`.\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipTier` description to document the GOLD rule.\n\nBoth the new `AcceptanceTest.coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
